🐛 Bug
Softplus operator backward implementation different from doc and forward implementation on applying threshold.
Documentation defines:
For numerical stability the implementation reverts to the linear function when input×β>threshold
https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.Softplus.html
This is consistent with the behavior of Softplus forward kernels. However on the backward computation,  the input * beta > threshold condition becomes output * beta> threshold. As shown in below code snippet from
master @ b39eeb07ed2f60a27b60ad32d172b58d31554282
// wrapper from ATen/native/Activation.cpp
Tensor softplus_backward(
    const Tensor& grad_output,
    const Tensor& self,
    const Scalar& beta,
    const Scalar& threshold,
    const Tensor& output) {
  Tensor grad_input;
  auto iter = TensorIterator::binary_op(grad_input, grad_output, output);
  softplus_backward_stub(iter.device_type(), iter, beta, threshold);
  return iter.output();
}

//CPU impl from ATen/native/cpu/Activation.cpp
cpu_kernel_vec(
        iter,
        [beta, threshold](scalar_t a, scalar_t b) -> scalar_t {
          scalar_t z = std::exp(b * beta);
          return (b * beta) > threshold ? a : a * (z - scalar_t(1.)) / z;
        },
        [beta_vec, one_vec, threshold_vec](Vec a, Vec b) -> Vec {
          const Vec z = (b * beta_vec).exp();
          return Vec::blendv(a * (z - one_vec) / z, a, (b * beta_vec) > threshold_vec);
        }
    );

// CUDA impl from ATen/native/cuda/Activation.cu
 AT_DISPATCH_FLOATING_TYPES_AND2(at::ScalarType::Half, at::ScalarType::BFloat16, iter.dtype(), "softplus_backward_cuda", [&]() {
    auto beta = beta_.to<scalar_t>();
    auto threshold = threshold_.to<scalar_t>();
    gpu_kernel(iter, [beta, threshold]GPU_LAMBDA(scalar_t a, scalar_t b) -> scalar_t {
      scalar_t z = std::exp(b * beta);
      return (b * beta) > threshold ? a : a * (z - scalar_t(1.)) / z;
    });
  });

This makes backward of Softplus op semantically mismatched with forward. Even though with the default threshold of 20 it may not have observable effect, it can break gradcheck when the threshold is lowered.
To Reproduce
Thanks @zasdfgbnm for the repro:
import torch
model = torch.nn.Softplus(threshold=1).double()
input = torch.tensor(0.9, dtype=torch.double, requires_grad=True)
output = model(input)
print(input.item(), output.item())
torch.autograd.gradcheck(model, input)

Threshold is really a method of improving numerical stability and current implementation shouldn't have problem with high threshold values (default 20 should be ok). It might make sense not to expose threshold as a parameter, as it is not a hyper-parameter to be tuned for sure. Updating all the softplus_backward to use the input to compute the gradients sounds like the the most thorough way.
This issue is intended to be used for some quick discussions. @zasdfgbnm , @mruberry
